WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.040
The two most important data governance roles are&nbsp;
data owners and data stewards, but there are other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.080
roles. When asked who has responsibility&nbsp;
for data governance in an organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.440
the answer must be everyone. Much like&nbsp;
requiring staff to agree to a code of ethics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.000
or adhere to particular values, if an&nbsp;
organization employs data governance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.320
the expectation must be that all staff agree&nbsp;
to support the function of data governance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.880
What other roles have particular&nbsp;
importance regarding data governance?

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.600
There are a range of technology roles, such as a&nbsp;
database administrator, or a software developer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.040
both of which, for example, manage many aspects&nbsp;
of creating, reading, updating and deleting data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.880
They may have special requirements relative to&nbsp;
data governance, such as adherence to industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.880
regulations. We know, for example, that everyone&nbsp;
who handles health care data must follow specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.800
data handling rules, like what can be stored and&nbsp;
who can access it. Other roles include attorneys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.560
who advise data owners and stewards about data&nbsp;
requirements, such as privacy or disclosure, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.440
are auditors who are tasked with making sure that&nbsp;
all of the organization's data is being handled in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.320
accordance with policies, and there's security&nbsp;
professionals who identify vulnerabilities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.280
the data security and confidentiality, and&nbsp;
make sure that all data is being kept out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.680
reach of those individuals both inside and&nbsp;
outside the organization who shouldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:37.920
access to it. There are also those people who&nbsp;
capture and input data. For example, sales clerks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.640
who handle credit cards and might have access&nbsp;
to customer's contact information, or telephone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.520
support agents who are working with your customers&nbsp;
to resolve any questions or issues they may have.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.080
When thinking about these roles, you&nbsp;
must think about the following questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.200
Are each of these roles sufficiently trained?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.000
What can be done to reduce data entry&nbsp;
errors and maintain high quality?

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.080
Finally, there's a critical role for management,&nbsp;
all levels of management must help to communicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.800
value and support enforcement. If&nbsp;
data governance is not supported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:21.840
and messaging from management isn't clear&nbsp;
or inconsistent, it will be less successful.

